

1️⃣ Prerequisites — Don’t skip these
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	🔥 You must have:
		• Git installed locally (Linux/Windows/macOS) and accessible from your terminal
		• A GitHub account
		• A repository created on GitHub where you will push this folder

2️⃣ OPTION 1 (EASIEST – NO COMMANDS)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	- Open your GitHub repo and click add file → Upload files
	- Drag your folder and click commit changes
	- This works, but this is not real Git usage. Fine for beginners

3️⃣ OPTION 2 (RECOMMENDED WAY)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Step 1: Create repository on GitHub
        - Open https://github.com
        - Click New repository
        - Give repo name and click create repository
        - Do NOT add README, .gitignore, license

    Step 2: Open folder on your computer
        - C:\Users\You\Desktop\MyFolder
        - Right-click inside the folder → Open Terminal / Command Prompt

4️⃣ Git Global Configuration (~/.gitconfig)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

	🔥 SET CONFIGURATION
		git config --global user.email "mr.digeshwar05@gmail.com"   → Sets the email address recorded in your commits globally (commit author email)
		git config --global user.name "Digeshwar"                   → Sets the name recorded as the commit author globally (commit author name)
		git config --global core.editor "code --wait"               → Makes VS Code Git's default editor. --wait makes Git wait until you close the editor before continuing.
		git config --global core.autocrlf "true"                    → Converts CRLF → LF when committing, but does not convert LF → CRLF when checking files out. Commonly used on Linux/macOS; on Windows, true is often used instead.
		git config --global --list                                  → Displays and Verify your current global Git configuration
	
	🔥 VERIFY CONFIGURATION	
		git config --global user.name                               → Check Git username used as the commit author
		git config --global user.email                  		    → Check Git email used as the commit author
		git config --global core.editor                  		    → Check Git default editor
		git config --global core.autocrlf                		    → Check line-ending configuration/conversion setting
		git config --global --get-regexp "user|core"     		    → Check all configured settings whose keys match user or core
	
	🔥 EDIT CONFIGURATION
		git config --global -e                                      → Opens the global Git configuration file in your configured editor.
	
	🔥 Credential Management
		git config --global --unset credential.helper               → Removes the globally configured/existing credential helper.
		git config --global credential.helper manager               → Configures Git Credential Manager to manage Git credentials.
	
	🔥 HTTP POST Buffer
		git config --global http.postBuffer 524288000               → Sets Git's HTTP POST buffer size to 500 MiB (Large Upload Support).
		git config --global --unset http.postBuffer                 → Removes the custom http.postBuffer setting and returns to Git's default behavior.
	
	🔥 Git Compression/Git large push fix (Don't blindly keep them in your global configuration)
		git config --global core.compression 0                      → Disables Git compression.
		git config --global --unset core.compression                → Removes the custom compression setting and returns to Git's default behavior.
	
	🔥 Slow HTTP Transfer Detection/Git large push fix (Don't blindly keep them in your global configuration)
		git config --global http.lowSpeedLimit 0                    → Sets the minimum transfer speed threshold to 0 bytes/sec (Don't cancel upload for slow upload speed).
		git config --global --unset http.lowSpeedLimit              → Removes the custom lowSpeedLimit setting and returns to the default.
		git config --global http.lowSpeedTime 999999                → Sets the amount of time Git waits when the HTTP transfer remains below the low-speed threshold (Give very long timeout to Git before Git gives up).
		git config --global --unset http.lowSpeedTime               → Removes the custom lowSpeedTime setting and returns to the default.


5️⃣ Work Management
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	
	🔥 Working Directory
	       │
	       │ git add     → Staging Area
	       │ git commit  → Local Repository (Commits)
	       │ git push    → Remote Repository (GitHub)


	🔥 Git File Stages
		U - Untracked                                               → File exists in your working directory but Git is not tracking/managing it yet.
		M - Modified												→ A tracked file has been changed after the last commit.
		D - Deleted 												→ A tracked file has been deleted from the working directory.
		A - Added to staging                                        → File has been added to the staging area using `git add`.
		S - Staged 													→ Changes are currently in the staging area and ready to be committed.
		C - Committed                                               → Changes have been saved in a commit in the local Git repository.


	🔥 Command You Need to Know
		git init                                                    → Initializes a new Git repository in the current directory (once per repository).
		git remote -v                                               → Shows the remote repositories configured for the local repository.
		git remote remove origin                                    → Removes the remote named origin from the local repository.
		git remote add origin YOUR_NEW_REPO_URL.git                 → Adds a remote repository and gives it the name origin.

	
	 🌿 Branch Management 
		git branch feature-login                                    → Creates a new branch called feature-login, but you remain on your current branch.
		git checkout develop                                        → Switches your working directory to another existing branch (Now you're working on the develop branch).
		git switch develop                                          → Modern Git prefers 👉 Switches your working directory to another existing branch (Now you're working on the develop branch).
		git checkout -b feature-login                               → Creates a new branch called feature-login and immediately switches to it.
		git switch -c feature-login                                 → Equivalent modern command (Creates a new branch called feature-login and immediately switches to it)
		
		git branch                                                  → Shows all local branches and marks the current branch with `*` that tells you which branch you're currently on.
		git branch --show-current                                   → Shows only the name of the current branch.
		git branch -m main                                          → Rename current branch to main.
		git branch -M main                                          → Force-renames the current branch to main, the -M option allows the rename even if a branch named main already exists.
	

		git ls-files | findstr assets                               → Lists Git-tracked file whose paths contain assets (already tracked files by Git).
		git rm -r --cached "docs"                                   → Optional: Removes the docs folder from Git's index (tracking) while keeping the files in your working directory (Usually you'd then add it to .gitignore: docs/).		
		git add User.java                                           → Stages a specific file for the next commit.
		git add User.java UserController.java                       → Stages both file for the next commit.
		git add .                                                   → Stages all changes under the current directory and its subdirectories including: New files, Modified files and Deleted files that are not excluded by .gitignore.
		git commit -m "first commit"                                → Creates a new commit from the changes currently in the staging area and -m Provide the commit message (The commit is stored locally, it does not upload anything to GitHub)
		git push -u origin main                                     → Firstly pushes the local main branch to the remote repository named origin and establishes an upstream tracking relationship.
		git push  												    → The -u is important for the first push because afterward you can usually simply use: git push (Future pushes)



		git status                                                  → Shows the current state of the working directory and staging area, including: U M D A S C
		git status --short                                          → Shows the same status in a compact two-column format using two-character XY codes:
																	XY file
																	││
																	│└── Working tree status
																	└─── Staging area (index) status

		git log                                                     → What commits exist 👉 Shows detailed commit history/Saved Point, including Commit ID (SHA), Author, Date, Commit message).
		git show                                                    → What did this particular commit change 👉 Shows the details of a specific commit. By default, it shows the latest commit (HEAD), including: Commit ID (SHA), Author, Date, Commit message, Changes introduced by that commit (diff)
		git log --oneline                                           → Shows the commit history/Saved Point in a compact, one-line-per-commit format.
		git show a82f91d                                            → Show a specific commit (Shows the details and changes of that particular commit).
		git show --stat                                             → Shows the commit information plus a summary of changed files, without the full diff.
		git show --name-only                                        → Shows the names of files changed by the commit.



		git checkout -- User.java                                   → Restore a file from another state 👉 Discards the unstaged changes in User.java and restores it from the current commit (HEAD): 👉 This can permanently discard your uncommitted changes.
		git restore User.java                                       → Modern Git prefers
		git checkout a82f91d                                        → Checkout a specific commit (Moves HEAD to that commit)

																	main                 git checkout B
																	  │
																	  A ── B ── C        A ── B ── C
																	            ↑             ↑
																	          branch         HEAD  👉 You're viewing/working from commit B, but you're not on a branch.
	 
		git checkout --orphan clean-main                            → Creates a new branch with no parent commit/history, while keeping the files from the current working tree (starts with a completely new history).
		git checkout --orphan clean-main                            → Creates a brand new branch (files remain but git history empty)
		git branch -d main                                          → Git refuses if deleting the branch would cause you to lose unmerged commits.
		git branch -D main                                          → Force-deletes the local main branch (-D can delete the branch even if it contains commits that haven't been merged.).


		_____________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________
		-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
		git reset <mode> HEAD~1										→ This moves HEAD back by one commit (from C → B), but what happens to the changes from C depends on the reset mode.
																	Before: A ── B ── C         After: A ── B
																	     		      ↑                     ↑
																	                 HEAD                  HEAD 

		+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
		|         Command              Commit C removed?       Changes of C kept?       Changes from C remain in staging area      Changes from C remain in Working files  |
		|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
		| git reset --soft HEAD~1   |   ✅ Removed       |   ✅ Kept                 |   ✅ Staged                            |   ✅ Kept                                |
		| git reset --mixed HEAD~1  |   ✅ Removed       |   ✅ Kept                 |   ❌ Changes from C are removed        |   ✅ Kept                                |
		| git reset --hard HEAD~1   |   ✅ Removed       |   ❌ Discarded            |   ❌ Changes from C are removed        |   ❌ Discarded                           |
		+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+	

